          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe about exercise and anxiety?",
          "answer": "The guidance I could retrieve says little about exercise for anxiety, and it does not grade how strong the evidence is.\n\n**What the sources say**\n\n- The generalized anxiety disorder summary (condition-generalized-anxiety-disorder) lists structured exercise among self-management approaches \"with evidence,\" alongside relaxation, mindfulness and guided digital programs. It does not give effect sizes or say how well exercise works compared with other options.\n- A mental health summary on physical activity and anxiety (lifestyle-generalized-anxiety-disorder-activity) makes three practical points. Starting small and keeping going matters more than an ambitious plan that gets abandoned. Exercise raises heart rate and breathing, which can feel like anxiety and may lead some people to avoid it. Knowing in advance that these sensations are expected can help.\n- The anxiety screening and first-line care guideline (guideline-anxiety-screening-and-first-line-care) covers screening, not exercise. Its retrieved text does not recommend or reject exercise.\n\n**What I could not find**\n\n- No systematic review, trial or effect size for exercise and anxiety was returned, so I cannot say how strong the evidence is.\n- No authority was found that disagrees with another about exercise for anxiety. The disagreement passage that came back concerned knee osteoarthritis treatments and does not apply here.\n- The other guidelines returned (osteoarthritis, hypertension, insomnia, COPD) concern other conditions. I have not used them as evidence for anxiety.\n\n**A safety note**\n\nIf exercise brings on chest pain, severe breathlessness, fainting or a racing heartbeat, stop and seek urgent medical care. A first panic attack with chest pain or breathlessness should also be assessed urgently, because the heart and lungs need checking. Anyone with known heart disease or very high blood pressure should see a clinician before starting vigorous activity.\n\nTo go further, a clinician or a more specialised review of exercise trials for anxiety would be the place to check the strength of the evidence.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
